# Product-Agnostic by Design

Per **D1**, this platform supports multiple products (NDB, DataLens, NCM,
future). No code may assume NDB. Everything product-specific reads from
`productService` config.

## What must be config-driven

| Item | Source of truth |
|---|---|
| JIRA project keys (`NDB`, `ERA`, `FEAT`) | `productService.getJiraProjects(productId)` |
| JIRA custom field IDs (`customfield_11067` etc.) | `productService.getCustomFields(productId)` |
| JIRA board IDs (`2888` etc.) | `productService.getBoards(productId)` |
| Confluence space keys | `productService.getConfluenceSpace(productId)` |
| GitHub orgs / repos | `productService.getGithubOrgs(productId)` |
| Audience overrides (per-product VP name, per-product audience presets) | `productService.getAudienceOverrides(productId)` |
| Release naming convention | `productService.getReleaseNamePattern(productId)` |
| Product-specific terminology (e.g. "NDB" vs "DataLens") | `productService.getDisplayName(productId)` |

## What may stay product-neutral

- Generic dates, ticket-key prefixes used **as variables**, audience role
  names (`vp`, `tpm`, `rm` — these are universal across products)
- Default JIRA hierarchy (X-FEAT → Feature → Epic → Story) — this is a
  Nutanix-wide convention, codified in `jira-date-hierarchy.mdc`
- Default audience presenter templates (overridable per product)

## Code patterns

### Correct

```typescript
// Get JIRA project list dynamically
const projects = await productService.getJiraProjects(productId);
const jql = `project in (${projects.join(',')}) AND ...`;

// Get custom field ID via lookup
const ccField = await productService.getCustomFields(productId).codeCompleteDate;

// Audience format for the right VP name
const audience = await productService.getAudienceOverrides(productId, 'vp');
const greeting = `Hi ${audience.recipientName},`;
```

### Forbidden

```typescript
// ❌ Hardcoded product key
const jql = `project = NDB AND ...`;

// ❌ Hardcoded custom field
const ccDate = issue.fields.customfield_11067;

// ❌ Hardcoded display name
console.log('NDB release status:');
```

## File-level rules

- **No string literal `"NDB"` outside `productService` config and tests.**
  Tests use NDB as a fixture; production code uses the active product from
  the user's session.
- **No string literal `customfield_NNNNN` outside `productService` config
  files.** (Per `minimal-architecture.mdc` hard line #3.)
- **Reports and emails reference the product by display name** from
  `productService.getDisplayName(productId)`.

## Multi-product session handling

- The web app's top bar has a product picker (D5). The picker writes to
  user session: `session.activeProducts = ['ndb', 'datalens']`
- Every Layer-3 service that reads data takes a `productIds` array — never
  assumes a single product
- The agent's orchestrator reads `session.activeProducts` at session start
  and includes it in every tool call
- "Active releases" (D13) are scoped to `session.activeProducts`

## Migration responsibility

When you touch a file containing hardcoded NDB strings:

1. If you're already editing it, move the strings to `productService` config
   in the same commit
2. If it's untouched but you reference it, file a `TODO(D1)` and pick it up
   in the next dedicated migration pass

Do not let the grandfathering tail grow.

## Never Do

- Add a new string literal `"NDB"` / `"ndb"` / `customfield_*` to non-config code
- Assume the active product without consulting `productService`
- Hard-code release names like `NDB-2.11` in generic services (they belong in
  config or are passed in as parameters)
- Build a report that only renders correctly for NDB

